<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Tübingen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1477</span> · Also known as Eberhard Karls University of Tübingen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a> was founded in 1477 by Count Eberhard V of Württemberg, called the Bearded, in his town of Tübingen. It became the university of the Duchy of Württemberg and, after the duchy adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1534, a centre of Lutheran theology. Its Protestant seminary, the Tübinger Stift, trained clergy and scholars, among them Hegel, Hölderlin and Schelling.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-tubingen.jpg" alt="University of Tübingen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prissantenbär · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uni_T%C3%BCbingen_Neue_Aula_Sommer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
